WEBVTT
TITLE Data Interpretation: Charts, Tables and Plots — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
One table, three years, four departments. Data interpretation asks one question of it: which two cells, and which ratio between them.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Step one: read the axes and the units. Intake is a count of students, the years are the columns, the departments are the rows.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
Step two: locate the two cells the sentence names, and build the total you need if it is not printed.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Step three: one ratio. Step four: check the direction — is the answer a share, a rate, a comparison, or an average?

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
What share of the 2025 intake was CSE? The part is 360. The total is that year’s 750, not the table’s 2,110.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
360 over 750 is 0.48, so 48.0 per cent. The four shares of 2025 are 48.0, 26.67, 17.33 and 8.0, and they sum to 100.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
A share is silent about size: 8 per cent of 750 is 60 students, and 8 per cent of a class of 50 is 4. The percentage is the same.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
So: the denominator is the phrase after "of". Say it before dividing, and the share question cannot go wrong.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
A pie draws each share as a slice of 360 degrees. CSE’s 48 per cent becomes 172.8 degrees.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
The four slices are 172.8, 96.0, 62.4 and 28.8 degrees, and they sum to 360 exactly — the same check as before, in degrees.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
But two pies with the same shares and different totals look identical. Only the total printed beside the pie tells you the amounts.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
So: a pie answers "what fraction", never "how many". Multiply the share by the printed total when the question asks for an amount.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
A rate of change is a difference over the value it started from: new minus old, divided by old.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
CSE 240 to 360 is 120 over 240, which is 50.0 per cent. ECE 180 to 200 is 20 over 180, which is 11.11 per cent.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
ME 150 to 130 is minus 13.33 per cent and CE 90 to 60 is minus 33.33 per cent. A minus sign is a fall, not a small rise.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
The total went 660 to 750, which is 13.64 per cent — and the parts sum to that: plus 120, plus 20, minus 20, minus 30 is plus 90.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
CSE’s share rose from 240 of 660, which is 36.36 per cent, to 360 of 750, which is 48.00 per cent.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
The difference is 11.64 percentage points. That is a subtraction of two percentages, and it is a change in points, not a change in per cent.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
As a per cent of the old share, the same move is 11.64 over 36.36, which is 32.0 per cent. Two true answers, two different questions.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
So: points subtract, per cent divides. Read which word the option uses before choosing between 11.64 and 32.0.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
In 2025, CSE is 360 and ECE is 200. CSE is 160 more than ECE, on a base of 200, so 80 per cent more.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Turn the sentence round: ECE is 160 less than CSE, on a base of 360, so 44.44 per cent less.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Check by going back: 200 times 1.8 is 360, and 360 times 0.5556 is 200. The ratio 1.8 is the same both ways; the percentages are not.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
So: the base is the word after "than". "More than ECE" divides by 200; "less than CSE" divides by 360.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
The four growth rates are plus 50.0, plus 11.11, minus 13.33 and minus 33.33 per cent. Their plain average is 3.61 per cent.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
But the data set grew from 660 to 750, which is 13.64 per cent. The plain average of the parts is not the growth of the whole.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Weighted by the 2023 counts, the same four rates give 13.64 per cent exactly — because a weighted average of growth rates is the growth of the total.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
So: average rates only with weights. If the question asks for the growth of the whole, use the two totals and divide once.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
What share of the 2024 intake was ECE? 190 over 700. That is 27.14 per cent, and the options are 26, 27, 28 and 30.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
Round the fraction, not the answer: 190 over 700 is 19 over 70, and 19 over 70 sits between 0.25 and 0.30, closer to 0.27.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
State the band: 27 per cent is 0.14 points low, which is about one student in 700. The estimate is safe.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
So: cancel zeros, compare against one half and one quarter, then say how far off you are. Never round the inputs before deciding what the question asks.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
Two bars for 2025: CSE 360 and CE 60. Drawn from zero, the heights are in the ratio 6 to 1.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Now start the axis at 50 instead of zero: the bars are 310 and 10, a visual ratio of 31 to 1, and the data has not changed.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
A stacked bar shows parts of one total. ME’s segment falls from 150 to 130, but its share falls further, from 22.73 to 17.33 per cent.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
So: read the axis before comparing bars, and read the total before comparing segments. Grouped bars compare parts; stacked bars compare wholes.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
The routine, three lines: name the four quantities the sentence contains; write the ratio in words; substitute and simplify.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Trap one: the wrong denominator. Trap two: points read as per cent. Both are caught by the words in line two.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Trap three: a plain average of rates. Trap four: a bar read without its axis. Both are caught by one question each.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
And the free checks: shares sum to 100, angles sum to 360, changes sum to the total change. Three additions that catch most wrong answers.

